            <ArticleTitle>Total systemic ropivacaine concentrations following aerosolized intraperitoneal delivery using the AeroSurge.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-24</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2013.07.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(13)00379-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate intraperitoneal ropivacaine delivery with the AeroSurge device in the clinical setting and to evaluate the total systemic ropivacaine levels achieved following delivery of 50 mg of aerosolized ropivacaine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Preliminary, prospective, nonrandomized study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Operating room of a university hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">5 consecutive ASA physical status 1 and 2 patients undergoing elective laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication or cholecystectomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Five mL of 1% ropivacaine was delivered through the 10 mm port using the AeroSurge device at peritoneal insufflation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Venous blood samples were collected and total ropivacaine concentration was determined using liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The AeroSurge device delivered ropivacaine, visible as mist within the peritoneal cavity. Peak concentration (Cmax) was attained between 10 and 30 minutes following the end of aerosolized ropivacaine delivery. At no stage did any level approach toxic levels.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This preliminary study confirms that aerosolized intraperitoneal local anesthetic is feasible, with ropivacaine concentrations remaining within safe levels.</AbstractText>
